# Mini SOC Platform — Component Contracts

## 1. Purpose

This document defines the shared data and API contracts used by the Mini SOC Platform.

The contracts covered in this document are:

* Common Event Model
* Alert Model
* Incident Model
* Backend API endpoints

These contracts are shared between the log collection, detection engine, backend API and dashboard components.

The common event format is the contract between components and must remain consistent throughout the project.

---

# 2. Common Event Model

Every raw log line is parsed by the collector into one normalized event structure before being stored in PostgreSQL.

## 2.1 Event structure

```json
{
  "event_id": "uuid",
  "timestamp": "2026-10-05T14:32:07Z",
  "host": "web-01",
  "log_type": "nginx_access",
  "category": "web",
  "action": "http_request",
  "src_ip": "192.168.20.45",
  "src_port": 51244,
  "dst_ip": "192.168.10.20",
  "dst_port": 80,
  "user": null,
  "outcome": "success",
  "details": {
    "method": "GET",
    "uri": "/dvwa/vulnerabilities/sqli/?id=1%27+OR+1%3D1",
    "status": 200,
    "user_agent": "sqlmap/1.8"
  },
  "raw": "original log line, unchanged"
}
```

## 2.2 Event fields

| Field       | Description                            |
| ----------- | -------------------------------------- |
| `event_id`  | Unique identifier for the event        |
* Parser tests use real sample log lines.
* Normalized events are stored in the PostgreSQL `events` table.
---

An alert represents a detection generated by the detection engine when a configured detection rule matches events.

* the detection rule
* when the activity was first observed
* when it was most recently observed
* how many matching events have been observed
* the events that triggered the alert

## 3.1 Alert requirements

The detection engine:

1. Evaluates new events against enabled rules.
2. Creates an alert when the rule condition is satisfied.
3. Links the alert to the event IDs that triggered it.
4. Updates an existing open alert when the same rule continues to match instead of creating duplicate alerts.
5. Updates the alert count and last-seen time when repeated matches occur.

## 3.2 Alert severity

The platform uses the following severity levels where specified by the detection rules:

* LOW
* MEDIUM
* HIGH
* CRITICAL

The severity of an alert comes from the detection rule or, for IDS alerts, is mapped from the Suricata priority.

## 3.3 Alert lifecycle

Alerts can be investigated by analysts and can be promoted to incidents.

The dashboard must allow analysts to:

* filter alerts
* change alert status
* promote an alert or attack chain to an incident

## 3.4 Alert Structure

 `alert_id`
 `rule_id`
 `title`
 `severity`
 `status`
 `first_seen`
 `last_seen`
 `src_ip`
 `dst_ip`
 `count`
 `chain_id`
 `assigned_to`
 `incident_id`

# 4. Incident Model

An incident represents a security investigation created from one or more related alerts.

The incident workflow supports the lifecycle:


## 4.1 Incident requirements

An incident must support:

* assignment of an owner
* status changes

* notes
* evidence
* response actions
* a timeline
* linked alerts
* reporting

## 4.2 Incident workflow

The incident lifecycle must support the statuses defined by the project specification.

An analyst can promote an alert or correlated attack chain to an incident, assign the incident and investigate it.

The incident timeline records relevant notes, evidence and response actions.

## 4.3 Incident Structure

 `incident_id`
 `title`
 `description`
 `severity`
 `status`
 `category`
 `assigned_to`
 `created_at`
 `closed_at`
 `root_cause`
 `lessons_learned`

# 5. Backend API Contract

The backend provides REST endpoints for authentication, event ingestion and searching, alert management, incident management, statistics and reporting.

## 5.1 Authentication

| Method | Endpoint          | Purpose      |
| ------ | ----------------- | ------------ |
| POST   | `/api/auth/login` | Obtain a JWT |

Passwords must be hashed using bcrypt or Argon2.

The system supports the following roles:

* `analyst`
* `lead`
* `admin`

The lead role can close incidents and manage users.

---

## 5.2 Event endpoints

| Method | Endpoint      | Purpose                     |
| ------ | ------------- | --------------------------- |
| POST   | `/api/ingest` | Agents submit event batches |
| GET    | `/api/events` | Search normalized events    |

The ingest endpoint uses API-key authentication.

The events endpoint supports searching normalized events.

---

## 5.3 Alert endpoints

| Method | Endpoint           | Purpose                           |
| ------ | ------------------ | --------------------------------- |
| GET    | `/api/alerts`      | List alerts                       |
| GET    | `/api/alerts/{id}` | View an individual alert          |
| PATCH  | `/api/alerts/{id}` | Change alert status or assignment |

Alerts must support filtering by:

* severity
* status
* rule
* IP
* time

---

## 5.4 Incident endpoints

| Method | Endpoint                       | Purpose                     |
| ------ | ------------------------------ | --------------------------- |
| POST   | `/api/incidents`               | Create an incident          |
| GET    | `/api/incidents/{id}`          | View an incident            |
| POST   | `/api/incidents/{id}/notes`    | Add an incident note        |
| POST   | `/api/incidents/{id}/evidence` | Upload incident evidence    |
| POST   | `/api/incidents/{id}/actions`  | Record a response action    |
| GET    | `/api/incidents/{id}/report`   | Generate an incident report |

---

## 5.5 Statistics endpoint

| Method | Endpoint     | Purpose                     |
| ------ | ------------ | --------------------------- |
| GET    | `/api/stats` | Return dashboard statistics |

---

# 6. Security Requirements

The backend must:

* authenticate users
* enforce user roles
* validate input on all endpoints
* hash passwords using bcrypt or Argon2
* maintain an append-only audit log for every write action
* keep secrets in `.env`
* never commit secrets to the repository

The platform itself must be tested for SQL injection, XSS and broken access control.

---

# 7. Evidence Contract

Evidence associated with an incident may include:

* log extracts
* screenshots
* packet captures
* notes

For uploaded evidence, the system must store:

* SHA-256 hash
* file size
* uploader
* upload time

Evidence files must be stored outside the web root and cannot be edited or overwritten.

A verification operation must be able to recompute the hash and determine whether the evidence has changed.

A chain-of-custody record must record every evidence view, download and verification.

---

# 8. Response Action Contract

Response actions record actions performed during incident response.

Each action records:

* action type
* target
* person who performed the action
* time
* reason
* result

Supported action types include:

* block IP
* disable account
* isolate host
* reset password
* patch
* other

Response actions appear on the incident timeline.

---

# 9. Audit Contract

Every write operation performed through the platform must generate an append-only audit record.

The audit record must identify the user, action and time associated with the operation.

---

# 10. Contract Change Policy

The event, alert, incident and API contracts should not be changed independently by one component.

Any change must be agreed by the relevant team members and reflected in this document before dependent components are updated.
Detection → Alert → Investigation → Incident → Response → Resolution → Report
---
* assign alerts
* view alerts
* the alert status
* the severity
